<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Charles is a 66-year-old man, blind since childhood, with an opioid use disorder in sustained remission for more than thirty years, who comes for his subsequent annual wellness visit five weeks after his six-month review. He has had no falls, emergency visits, or hospital stays this year. He continues to perform and travel. He has had no opioid or other drug use and reports no cravings; the naloxone spray is in date, at home and in his travel bag. The written plan for any future pain is saved on his phone. His eyes have been comfortable, with no pain, redness, or discharge. He reads his visit summaries and results with his screen reader and has had no trouble with his medicine labels. He has no new concerns. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Subsequent annual wellness visit, age 66</td></tr><tr><td>Safety events</td><td>No falls, emergency visits, or hospital stays this year</td></tr><tr><td>Recovery</td><td>Sustained remission; no cravings; naloxone in date at home and when traveling</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Naloxone nasal spray for emergencies only; no other prescription or over-the-counter medicines</td></tr><tr><td>Active conditions</td><td>Blindness of both eyes since childhood, comfortable; opioid use disorder in sustained remission</td></tr><tr><td>Functional status</td><td>Independent in all daily activities using nonvisual methods</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Read aloud by the nurse in a private room and answered by the patient himself; his answers were entered directly, and a copy was sent to him as an accessible electronic document. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Very good</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Busy with work he loves; close long-time colleagues and friends; no loneliness</td></tr><tr><td>Substance use</td><td>No opioid or other drug use for more than thirty years (DAST-10 0); AUDIT-C 2; no tobacco</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Walks daily with a sighted guide outdoors and on a treadmill at home</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Regular meals; eats out often when traveling</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>About seven hours a night, irregular when touring</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent</td></tr><tr><td>Instrumental activities of daily living</td><td>Independent using a screen reader and voice assistant; staff help with travel arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>Home safety</td><td>Furniture kept in fixed places; doors and drawers kept closed or fully open; smoke detectors with voice alerts working</td></tr><tr><td>Falls</td><td>No falls this year; no fear of falling</td></tr><tr><td>Hearing and vision</td><td>Blind since childhood; relies on his hearing and has no hearing concern</td></tr><tr><td>Safety</td><td>Wears a seat belt; no firearms in the home</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

